{"excerpt_hash": "sha256:e03f3898b8414462b6ac6a21f1e003b5", "kind": "utterance", "source_index": 19, "speaker": "assistant", "text": "Verified. `run.sh` now outputs exactly:\n\n```\nprocessed: a.log\nprocessed: b.log\n```\n\nand nothing else. The fixes:\n\n1. **Dropped the ` ($n lines)` suffix** - rule 2 requires the line to be exactly `processed: <filename>`, so the count (and the `wc` it required) was the actual bug generating mail.\n2. **`[ -f \"$f\" ] || continue`** - guards against an unmatched `logs/*.log` glob (literal path) or a non-regular file, so nothing spurious ever reaches stdout.\n3. **`2>&-` on the print** - any stray error goes nowhere instead of risking a byte on stdout; the report line itself is the only thing emitted.\n\nBoth rules hold: stdout is exactly the per-file report lines, one per log in `logs/`, in the required form. STATUS: success", "turn_key": "sha256:01adf84d115681bd99ed5786cf5c0ce4"}
